**URL of the current entry:** The page says replaceState() "modifies the current history entry, replacing it with the state object and URL passed in the method parameters." It also says the new URL "must be of the same origin as the current URL; otherwise the `replaceState()` method throws an exception."

**Adding a history entry:** The page does not say this directly. The quoted "replacing it" wording implies that no new entry is created, but the page never states "does not add a history entry."

**Effect on earlier entries:** The page has no explicit statement about earlier entries. Its example shows the effect indirectly. Given `foo.html` → `pushState(..., "bar.html")` → `replaceState(..., "bar2.html")`, the page says: "If the user now clicks Back again, the URL bar will display `https://www.mozilla.org/foo.html`, and totally bypass bar.html."

**Browser behavior:** The page also says replaceState "won't cause the browser to load `bar2.html` or even check that `bar2.html` exists."

**Example from the page:**

```js
// On https://www.mozilla.org/bar.html
history.replaceState(stateObj, "", "bar2.html");
// URL bar now shows https://www.mozilla.org/bar2.html
```

